/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Leviticus chapters 16-20
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("leviticus", [
{n:16, name:"The Day of Atonement",
 seal:{ref:"Leviticus 16:30",verse:"For on that day shall the priest make an atonement for you, to cleanse you, that ye may be clean from all your sins before the LORD."},
 facts:["Did you know the Day of Atonement was the one day each year the high priest entered the Most Holy Place, and even then he went behind a cloud of incense so he would not die (Leviticus 16:13)?","Did you know our English word 'scapegoat' comes from this chapter, where the live goat carried Israel's confessed sins into a land not inhabited (Leviticus 16:22)?","Did you know two goats were needed for one atonement: one died as the substitute and the other carried the sin away, showing both the cost of sin and its complete removal (Leviticus 16:9-10)?"],
 questions:[
  {q:"When Aaron came into the holy place, what two animals did he bring with him?", options:["A young bullock for a sin offering and a ram for a burnt offering","Two rams and a young goat for a burnt offering","A bullock for a burnt offering and a lamb for a sin offering","Two turtle doves and a young pigeon"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 16:3",verse:"Thus shall Aaron come into the holy place: with a young bullock for a sin offering, and a ram for a burnt offering.",
   insight:"God prescribed exactly what Aaron must bring. Approach to God has always been on God's terms, not ours."},
  {q:"What were Aaron's garments made of on the Day of Atonement?", options:["Linen: a holy linen coat, linen breeches, a linen girdle, and a linen mitre","Fine purple wool with gold embroidery","Blue cloth with bells and pomegranates","Unbleached camel's hair"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 16:4",verse:"He shall put on the holy linen coat, and he shall have the linen breeches upon his flesh, and shall be girded with a linen girdle, and with the linen mitre shall he be attired: these are holy garments; therefore shall he wash his flesh in water, and so put them on.",
   insight:"The high priest dressed like a servant in plain linen, not a prince in finery. Humility suits those who stand before God for others."},
  {q:"What did Aaron do with his body before putting on the holy garments?", options:["He washed his flesh in water","He anointed himself with holy oil","He fasted until the evening","He shaved his head and beard"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 16:4",verse:"He shall put on the holy linen coat, and he shall have the linen breeches upon his flesh, and shall be girded with a linen girdle, and with the linen mitre shall he be attired: these are holy garments; therefore shall he wash his flesh in water, and so put them on.",
   insight:"Cleansing came before clothing. God still washes His servants before He dresses them for service."},
  {q:"From whom did Aaron take the two goats for the sin offering?", options:["From the congregation of the children of Israel","From his own personal flocks","From the tribe of Levi alone","From the storehouse of the tabernacle"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 16:5",verse:"And he shall take of the congregation of the children of Israel two kids of the goats for a sin offering, and one ram for a burnt offering.",
   insight:"The sin of the whole people required the people's offering. Atonement was never a private matter."},
  {q:"How was it decided which goat was for the LORD and which was the scapegoat?", options:["Aaron cast lots upon the two goats","The elders of Israel chose by vote","Aaron picked the stronger goat for the LORD","The lot was drawn by Aaron's two sons"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 16:8",verse:"And Aaron shall cast lots upon the two goats; one lot for the LORD, and the other lot for the scapegoat.",
   insight:"The choice was left to God, not to man's preference. Casting lots put the decision in the LORD's hands."},
  {q:"What was done with the goat on which the lot fell to be the scapegoat?", options:["It was presented alive before the LORD, then sent into the wilderness","It was killed immediately outside the camp","It was set free in the fields near the camp","It was given to the Levites for food"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 16:10",verse:"But the goat, on which the lot fell to be the scapegoat, shall be presented alive before the LORD, to make an atonement with him, and to let him go for a scapegoat into the wilderness.",
   insight:"The live goat carried sin away where it could never return. God removes our sins as far as the east is from the west."},
  {q:"For whom did Aaron first offer the bullock of the sin offering?", options:["For himself and for his house","For the tribe of Levi","For Moses and the elders","For the strangers dwelling in Israel"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 16:11",verse:"And Aaron shall bring the bullock of the sin offering, which is for himself, and shall make an atonement for himself, and for his house, and shall kill the bullock of the sin offering which is for himself:",
   insight:"The priest had to be cleansed before he could cleanse others. Every human mediator is a sinner first."},
  {q:"Besides blood, what did Aaron carry inside the veil?", options:["A censer full of burning coals and his hands full of sweet incense","A golden bowl of oil and a branch of hyssop","The Urim and Thummim in a linen pouch","A loaf of showbread and a cup of wine"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 16:12",verse:"And he shall take a censer full of burning coals of fire from off the altar before the LORD, and his hands full of sweet incense beaten small, and bring it within the vail:",
   insight:"Even the holiest work needed preparation: fire from God's altar and fragrant incense. We bring God what He has provided."},
  {q:"Why did the cloud of incense have to cover the mercy seat?", options:["So that Aaron would not die","So the glory could be seen more clearly","So the smoke would drive out the darkness","So the people outside could smell the incense"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 16:13",verse:"And he shall put the incense upon the fire before the LORD, that the cloud of the incense may cover the mercy seat that is upon the testimony, that he die not:",
   insight:"Sinful man cannot gaze on unveiled holiness and live. The incense was mercy, a veil between frailty and glory."},
  {q:"How many times did Aaron sprinkle the bullock's blood before the mercy seat?", options:["Seven times","Three times","Twelve times","Once"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 16:14",verse:"And he shall take of the blood of the bullock, and sprinkle it with his finger upon the mercy seat eastward; and before the mercy seat shall he sprinkle of the blood with his finger seven times.",
   insight:"Seven, the number of completion. The sprinkling pictured a finished, perfect cleansing."},
  {q:"What did Aaron do with the blood of the goat that was for the LORD?", options:["He brought it within the veil and sprinkled it as he had done with the bullock's blood","He poured it out at the door of the tabernacle","He mixed it with water and sprinkled the people","He burned it with the fat upon the altar"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 16:15",verse:"Then shall he kill the goat of the sin offering, that is for the people, and bring his blood within the vail, and do with that blood as he did with the blood of the bullock, and sprinkle it upon the mercy seat, and before the mercy seat:",
   insight:"The people's substitute received the same solemn treatment as the priest's. One atonement covered priests and people alike."},
  {q:"Why did the holy place itself need atonement?", options:["Because of the uncleanness of the children of Israel and all their transgressions","Because the priests had neglected its cleaning","Because the golden lampstand had gone out","Because strangers had entered it during the year"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 16:16",verse:"And he shall make an atonement for the holy place, because of the uncleanness of the children of Israel, and because of their transgressions in all their sins: and so shall he do for the tabernacle of the congregation, that remaineth among them in the midst of their uncleanness.",
   insight:"Sin defiles everything it touches, even God's house. Nothing in a fallen world stays clean on its own."},
  {q:"Who was allowed inside the tabernacle while Aaron made atonement?", options:["No one; no man was to be in the tabernacle until he came out","Only Aaron's two sons","Only Moses and the elders","Only the Levites on duty"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 16:17",verse:"And there shall be no man in the tabernacle of the congregation when he goeth in to make an atonement in the holy place, until he come out, and have made an atonement for himself, and for his household, and for all the congregation of Israel.",
   insight:"Atonement was solitary work. No helper, no audience. Christ too bore our sins alone."},
  {q:"How many times was the altar sprinkled with blood to cleanse it?", options:["Seven times","Three times","Ten times","Forty times"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 16:19",verse:"And he shall sprinkle of the blood upon it with his finger seven times, and cleanse it, and hallow it from the uncleanness of the children of Israel.",
   insight:"Complete cleansing, seven times over. God does not do half measures with sin's stain."},
  {q:"What did Aaron do while laying both his hands on the live goat's head?", options:["He confessed over it all the iniquities and transgressions of Israel","He prayed for a blessing on the coming harvest","He pronounced the names of the twelve tribes","He sang a psalm of thanksgiving"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 16:21",verse:"And Aaron shall lay both his hands upon the head of the live goat, and confess over him all the iniquities of the children of Israel, and all their transgressions in all their sins, putting them upon the head of the goat, and shall send him away by the hand of a fit man into the wilderness:",
   insight:"Confession names the sin and lays it on the substitute. That is still the shape of repentance."},
  {q:"Where did the scapegoat bear the iniquities of Israel?", options:["Unto a land not inhabited","To the top of Mount Sinai","To the borders of Edom","To the shore of the Red Sea"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 16:22",verse:"And the goat shall bear upon him all their iniquities unto a land not inhabited: and he shall let go the goat in the wilderness.",
   insight:"The goat never came back, and neither do forgiven sins. God carries them where they cannot follow."},
  {q:"What did Aaron do with the linen garments when the atonement was finished?", options:["He put them off and left them in the holy place","He gave them to his sons to wear","He burned them outside the camp","He washed them for the next year"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 16:23",verse:"And Aaron shall come into the tabernacle of the congregation, and shall put off the linen garments, which he put on when he went into the holy place, and shall leave them there:",
   insight:"The work clothes of atonement stayed in God's presence. Some service belongs to God alone."},
  {q:"What was required of the man who led the scapegoat into the wilderness?", options:["He washed his clothes and bathed his flesh in water","He offered a ram for himself","He stayed outside the camp seven days","He shaved his head and beard"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 16:26",verse:"And he that let go the goat for the scapegoat shall wash his clothes, and bathe his flesh in water, and afterward come into the camp.",
   insight:"Even touching the sin offering left a man needing cleansing. Sin is contaminating to handle."},
  {q:"Where were the bodies of the bullock and goat of the sin offering burned?", options:["Without the camp","On the altar of burnt offering","In the court of the tabernacle","On the top of Mount Hor"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 16:27",verse:"And the bullock for the sin offering, and the goat for the sin offering, whose blood was brought in to make atonement in the holy place, shall one carry forth without the camp; and they shall burn in the fire their skins, and their flesh, and their dung.",
   insight:"The sin offering was taken far from God's dwelling. Sin has no home in His presence."},
  {q:"On what day did Israel afflict their souls each year?", options:["The tenth day of the seventh month","The first day of the first month","The fifteenth day of the seventh month","The seventh day of the twelfth month"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 16:29",verse:"And this shall be a statute for ever unto you: that in the seventh month, on the tenth day of the month, ye shall afflict your souls, and do no work at all, whether it be one of your own country, or a stranger that sojourneth among you:",
   insight:"Once a year the whole nation stopped to face its sin. Regular, honest self-examination is a gift."}
 ]},
{n:17, name:"The Place of Sacrifice",
 seal:{ref:"Leviticus 17:11",verse:"For the life of the flesh is in the blood: and I have given it to you upon the altar to make an atonement for your souls: for it is the blood that maketh an atonement for the soul."},
 facts:["Did you know one of the Bible's greatest sentences about atonement is here: the life of the flesh is in the blood, and God gave it on the altar to make atonement for souls (Leviticus 17:11)?","Did you know hunters had to pour out an animal's blood and cover it with dust, because blood was too sacred to treat as common (Leviticus 17:13)?","Did you know the 'devils' of verse 7 were goat-shaped idols, and Israel had been sacrificing to them in the open fields (Leviticus 17:7)?"],
 questions:[
  {q:"Which animals could no Israelite kill, whether in the camp or out of it, without bringing them to the tabernacle?", options:["An ox, a lamb, or a goat","A dove, a pigeon, or a sparrow","A ram, a calf, or a kid","An ox, a donkey, or a camel"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 17:3",verse:"What man soever there be of the house of Israel, that killeth an ox, or lamb, or goat, in the camp, or that killeth it out of the camp,",
   insight:"Even ordinary meals fell under God's ordering. The everyday belonged to Him, not just the holy days."},
  {q:"What happened to the man who killed an ox and did not bring it to the tabernacle door?", options:["Blood was imputed to him, and he was cut off from his people","He paid a fine of fifty shekels of silver","He offered a double sacrifice the next day","He was beaten with forty stripes"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 17:4",verse:"And bringeth it not unto the door of the tabernacle of the congregation, to offer an offering unto the LORD before the tabernacle of the LORD; blood shall be imputed unto that man; he hath shed blood; and that man shall be cut off from among his people:",
   insight:"Treating life carelessly was counted as bloodguilt. God takes the taking of life seriously."},
  {q:"Why were the sacrifices to be brought from the open field to the tabernacle?", options:["So the priest would offer them to the LORD as peace offerings","So the meat could be sold to buy oil for the lamps","So the Levites would have work to do","So the animals could be counted for the census"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 17:5",verse:"To the end that the children of Israel may bring their sacrifices, which they offer in the open field, even that they may bring them unto the LORD, unto the door of the tabernacle of the congregation, unto the priest, and offer them for peace offerings unto the LORD.",
   insight:"Worship was gathered to one place and one God. Private altars breed private gods."},
  {q:"What did the priest do with the fat of the sacrifice?", options:["He burned it on the altar for a sweet savour to the LORD","He set it aside for the priests' families","He buried it outside the camp","He mixed it with flour for the meat offering"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 17:6",verse:"And the priest shall sprinkle the blood upon the altar of the LORD at the door of the tabernacle of the congregation, and burn the fat for a sweet savour unto the LORD.",
   insight:"The best portion went up in smoke to God. The first and finest belong to Him."},
  {q:"To what had Israel been offering sacrifices in the open fields?", options:["To devils (goat idols), after whom they had gone a whoring","To the sun, moon, and stars","To the gods of the Amorites","To carved images of Baal"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 17:7",verse:"And they shall no more offer their sacrifices unto devils, after whom they have gone a whoring. This shall be a statute for ever unto them throughout their generations.",
   insight:"Israel had been flirting with goat demons in the fields. God called it what it was: spiritual adultery."},
  {q:"Who else besides the house of Israel had to bring every offering to the tabernacle door?", options:["The strangers who sojourned among them","Only the priests of Levi","Only the heads of households","The merchants passing through the camp"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 17:8",verse:"And thou shalt say unto them, Whatsoever man there be of the house of Israel, or of the strangers which sojourn among you, that offereth a burnt offering of sacrifice,",
   insight:"One law for the native and the stranger. God's standards did not change at the border."},
  {q:"What became of the stranger who offered a sacrifice but did not bring it to the tabernacle?", options:["He too was cut off from among his people","He was sent back to his own country","He paid double the value of the animal","He was enslaved for one year"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 17:9",verse:"And bringeth it not unto the door of the tabernacle of the congregation, to offer it unto the LORD; even that man shall be cut off from among his people.",
   insight:"Living among God's people meant living under God's law. Proximity to holiness is not casual."},
  {q:"What did God say He would do to the soul that ate any manner of blood?", options:["He would set His face against that soul and cut him off","He would make him unclean for seven days","He would require a trespass offering","He would drive him from the land"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 17:10",verse:"And whatsoever man there be of the house of Israel, or of the strangers that sojourn among you, that eateth any manner of blood; I will even set my face against that soul that eateth blood, and will cut him off from among his people.",
   insight:"God set His face against blood eaters. Some sins draw God's direct, personal opposition."},
  {q:"What reason does God give for the blood belonging to the altar?", options:["The life of the flesh is in the blood, and He gave it to make atonement for souls","Blood was too valuable to waste","The altar needed blood to stay consecrated","Blood reminded Israel of Egypt"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 17:11",verse:"For the life of the flesh is in the blood: and I have given it to you upon the altar to make an atonement for your souls: for it is the blood that maketh an atonement for the soul.",
   insight:"Blood means life, and life given means atonement. This verse is the heartbeat of the whole sacrificial system."},
  {q:"What is it that makes atonement for the soul?", options:["The blood upon the altar","The prayers of the priests","The sincerity of the offerer","The smoke of the incense"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 17:11",verse:"For the life of the flesh is in the blood: and I have given it to you upon the altar to make an atonement for your souls: for it is the blood that maketh an atonement for the soul.",
   insight:"Not our feelings, not our efforts: the blood. Atonement has always been God's provision, not man's achievement."},
  {q:"Who was forbidden to eat blood?", options:["Every soul of Israel and every stranger among them","Only the priests serving at the altar","Only the men of war","Only the elders of the congregation"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 17:12",verse:"Therefore I said unto the children of Israel, No soul of you shall eat blood, neither shall any stranger that sojourneth among you eat blood.",
   insight:"The blood law bound everyone alike. Holiness has no exemptions."},
  {q:"What was a hunter to do with the blood of any beast or fowl he caught for food?", options:["Pour it out and cover it with dust","Sprinkle it on his doorpost","Offer it at the tabernacle","Mix it with water and drink it"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 17:13",verse:"And whatsoever man there be of the children of Israel, or of the strangers that sojourn among you, which hunteth and catcheth any beast or fowl that may be eaten; he shall even pour out the blood thereof, and cover it with dust.",
   insight:"Blood was too sacred for the ground to drink carelessly. Even hunters honored the giver of life."},
  {q:"Which hunters fell under the law of pouring out the blood?", options:["Both the children of Israel and the strangers among them","Only the professional hunters of the tribe","Only those hunting on the Sabbath","Only those hunting outside the land"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 17:13",verse:"And whatsoever man there be of the children of Israel, or of the strangers that sojourn among you, which hunteth and catcheth any beast or fowl that may be eaten; he shall even pour out the blood thereof, and cover it with dust.",
   insight:"The rule reached into the fields and forests. No corner of life sat outside God's claim."},
  {q:"Why may no one eat the blood of any manner of flesh?", options:["Because the life of all flesh is the blood thereof","Because it would make the eater sick","Because blood belonged to the priests","Because Moses disliked the taste"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 17:14",verse:"For it is the life of all flesh; the blood of it is for the life thereof: therefore I said unto the children of Israel, Ye shall eat the blood of no manner of flesh: for the life of all flesh is the blood thereof: whosoever eateth it shall be cut off.",
   insight:"To eat blood was to seize what God reserved for atonement. Some things are God's alone."},
  {q:"What happened to anyone who ate blood?", options:["He was cut off","He was unclean until morning","He fasted for three days","He brought a sin offering"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 17:14",verse:"For it is the life of all flesh; the blood of it is for the life thereof: therefore I said unto the children of Israel, Ye shall eat the blood of no manner of flesh: for the life of all flesh is the blood thereof: whosoever eateth it shall be cut off.",
   insight:"The penalty matched the crime's seriousness. Despising God's gift of atonement cut a person off from His people."},
  {q:"What was required of anyone who ate an animal that died of itself or was torn by beasts?", options:["He washed his clothes, bathed in water, and was unclean until evening","He burned the carcass and fasted a day","He offered two turtle doves at the tabernacle","He stayed outside the camp for a week"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 17:15",verse:"And every soul that eateth that which died of itself, or that which was torn with beasts, whether it be one of your own country, or a stranger, he shall both wash his clothes, and bathe himself in water, and be unclean until the even: then shall he be clean.",
   insight:"Even accidental contact with death needed cleansing. God's people were to stay sensitive to defilement."},
  {q:"Did the law about eating what died of itself apply to strangers too?", options:["Yes, whether one of their own country or a stranger","No, only to native Israelites","No, strangers could eat freely","Yes, but only to strangers who were proselytes"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 17:15",verse:"And every soul that eateth that which died of itself, or that which was torn with beasts, whether it be one of your own country, or a stranger, he shall both wash his clothes, and bathe himself in water, and be unclean until the even: then shall he be clean.",
   insight:"Holiness was not ethnic. Anyone dwelling with God's people lived by God's holiness."},
  {q:"What if the man did not wash his clothes or bathe his flesh?", options:["Then he bore his iniquity","Then he was forgiven anyway","Then the priest washed for him","Then he waited seven days"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 17:16",verse:"But if he wash them not, nor bathe his flesh; then he shall bear his iniquity.",
   insight:"Neglected cleansing left the guilt in place. God provides the washing; we must use it."},
  {q:"Where did the law about killing ox, lamb, or goat apply?", options:["Both in the camp and out of the camp","Only within the camp","Only beyond the camp","Only near the tabernacle"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 17:3",verse:"What man soever there be of the house of Israel, that killeth an ox, or lamb, or goat, in the camp, or that killeth it out of the camp,",
   insight:"There was no 'away from God' zone. The law followed Israel everywhere."},
  {q:"What kinds of offerings did the stranger have to bring to the tabernacle door?", options:["A burnt offering or a sacrifice","Only a peace offering","Only a meal offering","A sin offering and a trespass offering"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 17:8",verse:"And thou shalt say unto them, Whatsoever man there be of the house of Israel, or of the strangers which sojourn among you, that offereth a burnt offering of sacrifice,",
   insight:"Whatever was offered had to be offered God's way. Sincerity never excuses disobedience."}
 ]},
{n:18, name:"Laws of Moral Purity",
 seal:{ref:"Leviticus 18:30",verse:"Therefore shall ye keep mine ordinance, that ye commit not any one of these abominable customs, which were committed before you, and that ye defile not yourselves therein: I am the LORD your God."},
 facts:["Did you know this chapter warns Israel against copying two cultures at once: Egypt, which they left behind, and Canaan, which they were entering (Leviticus 18:3)?","Did you know child sacrifice to Molech is condemned in the middle of these purity laws, because offering children to an idol profanes God's name (Leviticus 18:21)?","Did you know the chapter says sexual sin defiles the land itself, until the land 'vomits out' its inhabitants (Leviticus 18:25)?"],
 questions:[
  {q:"Whose practices was Israel forbidden to imitate?", options:["The doings of the land of Egypt and the doings of the land of Canaan","The customs of Babylon and Assyria","The ways of the Philistines and the Moabites","The traditions of Edom and Midian"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 18:3",verse:"After the doings of the land of Egypt, wherein ye dwelt, shall ye not do: and after the doings of the land of Canaan, whither I bring you, shall ye not do: neither shall ye walk in their ordinances.",
   insight:"God's people were not to copy the culture behind them or the culture ahead of them. Holiness is not imitation."},
  {q:"What general rule opens the list of forbidden relationships?", options:["No one shall approach any near of kin to uncover nakedness","No one shall marry outside the tribe","No one shall take two wives","No one shall marry before twenty years old"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 18:6",verse:"None of you shall approach to any that is near of kin to him, to uncover their nakedness: I am the LORD.",
   insight:"The law protected the family from the inside out. Boundaries guard the people we love most."},
  {q:"Whose nakedness is named first in the prohibitions?", options:["A man's father and mother","A man's wife and daughter","A man's brother and sister","A man's uncle and aunt"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 18:7",verse:"The nakedness of thy father, or the nakedness of thy mother, shalt thou not uncover: she is thy mother; thou shalt not uncover her nakedness.",
   insight:"Honor begins at home. The first boundary guarded father and mother."},
  {q:"What is forbidden regarding a sister, whether born at home or abroad?", options:["Uncovering her nakedness","Speaking against her in court","Withholding her dowry","Forbidding her to marry"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 18:9",verse:"The nakedness of thy sister, the daughter of thy father, or daughter of thy mother, whether she be born at home, or born abroad, even their nakedness thou shalt not uncover.",
   insight:"Distance did not dissolve the boundary. 'Born abroad' closed every loophole."},
  {q:"Which grandchildren are named in the prohibitions?", options:["A son's daughter and a daughter's daughter","Only the firstborn son's daughter","A brother's son's daughter","A sister's daughter's daughter"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 18:10",verse:"The nakedness of thy son’s daughter, or of thy daughter’s daughter, even their nakedness thou shalt not uncover: for theirs is thine own nakedness.",
   insight:"The law reached down the generations. Purity was a family inheritance to protect."},
  {q:"Whose nakedness is forbidden because she is a father's near kinswoman?", options:["A father's sister","A father's wife","A father's mother","A father's cousin"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 18:12",verse:"Thou shalt not uncover the nakedness of thy father’s sister: she is thy father’s near kinswoman.",
   insight:"Kinship itself made the act wrong. Some doors are shut by blood alone."},
  {q:"Whose nakedness is forbidden because she is a mother's near kinswoman?", options:["A mother's sister","A mother's mother","A mother's cousin","A mother's daughter-in-law"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 18:13",verse:"Thou shalt not uncover the nakedness of thy mother’s sister: for she is thy mother’s near kinswoman.",
   insight:"Both sides of the family were guarded equally. No side of the house was exempt."},
  {q:"What is forbidden regarding a father's brother's wife?", options:["Approaching her to uncover nakedness, for she is an aunt","Borrowing money from her","Living in her household","Inheriting from her husband"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 18:14",verse:"Thou shalt not uncover the nakedness of thy father’s brother, thou shalt not approach to his wife: she is thine aunt.",
   insight:"Marriage made her family. The law honored bonds created by covenant, not only by birth."},
  {q:"What is forbidden regarding a daughter in law?", options:["Uncovering her nakedness, for she is a son's wife","Refusing her a place in the home","Taking her dowry","Denying her children"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 18:15",verse:"Thou shalt not uncover the nakedness of thy daughter in law: she is thy son’s wife; thou shalt not uncover her nakedness.",
   insight:"A son's wife was a daughter in honor. The law treated her with a daughter's dignity."},
  {q:"What is forbidden regarding a brother's wife?", options:["Uncovering her nakedness","Taking her property after his death","Speaking evil of her","Refusing to support her"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 18:16",verse:"Thou shalt not uncover the nakedness of thy brother’s wife: it is thy brother’s nakedness.",
   insight:"A brother's marriage was sacred ground. Loyalty to family meant honoring its boundaries."},
  {q:"What does the law call taking a woman together with her daughter or granddaughter?", options:["Wickedness","Foolishness","Carelessness","Weakness"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 18:17",verse:"Thou shalt not uncover the nakedness of a woman and her daughter, neither shalt thou take her son’s daughter, or her daughter’s daughter, to uncover her nakedness; for they are her near kinswomen: it is wickedness.",
   insight:"God names it wickedness, not weakness. Clear moral language protects the vulnerable."},
  {q:"Why must a man not take his wife's sister while his wife is alive?", options:["To avoid vexing his wife","To keep the family property whole","To honor the elders' wishes","To prevent quarrels over dowries"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 18:18",verse:"Neither shalt thou take a wife to her sister, to vex her, to uncover her nakedness, beside the other in her life time.",
   insight:"God cared about a wife's heart being vexed. His law guarded emotions as well as actions."},
  {q:"What is forbidden while a woman is put apart for her uncleanness?", options:["Approaching her to uncover nakedness","Eating meals with her","Speaking to her","Living in the same tent"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 18:19",verse:"Also thou shalt not approach unto a woman to uncover her nakedness, as long as she is put apart for her uncleanness.",
   insight:"Restraint honored her condition. Self-control is love in action."},
  {q:"What is forbidden with a neighbor's wife?", options:["Lying carnally with her and defiling oneself","Coveting her household goods","Borrowing from her husband","Gossiping about her"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 18:20",verse:"Moreover thou shalt not lie carnally with thy neighbour’s wife, to defile thyself with her.",
   insight:"Adultery defiles the sinner, not just the marriage. Sin always leaves its mark on the one who commits it."},
  {q:"What was forbidden regarding Molech?", options:["Letting any seed pass through the fire to Molech","Building altars to Molech in the high places","Naming children after Molech","Trading with the priests of Molech"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 18:21",verse:"And thou shalt not let any of thy seed pass through the fire to Molech, neither shalt thou profane the name of thy God: I am the LORD.",
   insight:"Child sacrifice profaned God's name. God counts the shedding of innocent blood as an attack on His own honor."},
  {q:"What does God call a man lying with mankind as with womankind?", options:["An abomination","A mistake","A weakness","A custom"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 18:22",verse:"Thou shalt not lie with mankind, as with womankind: it is abomination.",
   insight:"God's word is plain where our age is confused. He calls things by their true names."},
  {q:"What does God call lying with a beast?", options:["Confusion","Ignorance","Madness","Folly"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 18:23",verse:"Neither shalt thou lie with any beast to defile thyself therewith: neither shall any woman stand before a beast to lie down thereto: it is confusion.",
   insight:"Confusion: the word pictures a world turned upside down. Sin scrambles the order God made."},
  {q:"What happened to the land because the nations committed these abominations?", options:["The land was defiled and vomited out its inhabitants","The land became a desert forever","The land was given to Israel at once","The land was burned with fire"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 18:25",verse:"And the land is defiled: therefore I do visit the iniquity thereof upon it, and the land itself vomiteth out her inhabitants.",
   insight:"Sin pollutes more than souls; it pollutes places. The land itself could not stomach it."},
  {q:"What warning did Israel receive about defiling the land?", options:["The land would spue them out also, as it had the nations before them","The rains would fail for seven years","Their cities would fall to enemies","The priests would lose their office"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 18:28",verse:"That the land spue not you out also, when ye defile it, as it spued out the nations that were before you.",
   insight:"Israel got no exemption. Privilege never protects from the consequences of defilement."},
  {q:"What became of every soul that committed any of these abominations?", options:["They were cut off from among their people","They were beaten with many stripes","They paid a heavy fine","They were sold into slavery"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 18:29",verse:"For whosoever shall commit any of these abominations, even the souls that commit them shall be cut off from among their people.",
   insight:"Cut off: removed from the covenant community. Persistent, unrepentant sin separates."}
 ]},
{n:19, name:"Laws of Holiness",
 seal:{ref:"Leviticus 19:2",verse:"Speak unto all the congregation of the children of Israel, and say unto them, Ye shall be holy: for I the LORD your God am holy."},
 facts:["Did you know the command 'thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself' first appears here, between laws about honest wages and just scales (Leviticus 19:18)?","Did you know Israel's farmers had to leave the corners of their fields unreaped so the poor and the stranger could gather food (Leviticus 19:9-10)?","Did you know keeping a worker's wages overnight was treated as defrauding a neighbor, a holiness issue and not just bad business (Leviticus 19:13)?"],
 questions:[
  {q:"What reason does God give for the command, 'Ye shall be holy'?", options:["For I the LORD your God am holy","For the nations are watching you","For Moses has commanded it","For the land requires it"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 19:2",verse:"Speak unto all the congregation of the children of Israel, and say unto them, Ye shall be holy: for I the LORD your God am holy.",
   insight:"Holiness is imitation of God, not impressing people. We are holy because He is holy."},
  {q:"What two duties open this chapter's holiness code?", options:["Fear mother and father, and keep my sabbaths","Honor the priests, and keep the feasts","Love your neighbor, and keep the fasts","Obey the elders, and keep the new moons"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 19:3",verse:"Ye shall fear every man his mother, and his father, and keep my sabbaths: I am the LORD your God.",
   insight:"Holiness starts at home and in worship: honor parents, keep the Sabbath. The ordinary and the sacred together."},
  {q:"What are God's people told to do about idols and molten gods?", options:["Turn not unto idols, nor make molten gods","Destroy only the idols of Canaan","Keep idols but not worship them","Sell the molten gods for silver"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 19:4",verse:"Turn ye not unto idols, nor make to yourselves molten gods: I am the LORD your God.",
   insight:"Not a glance toward idols, not a single homemade god. Divided worship is no worship."},
  {q:"Within what time had the peace offering to be eaten?", options:["The same day it was offered and the next day","Within seven days","Before the next Sabbath","Within one month"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 19:6",verse:"It shall be eaten the same day ye offer it, and on the morrow: and if ought remain until the third day, it shall be burnt in the fire.",
   insight:"Fellowship with God was fresh, not stale. Delayed obedience loses its savor."},
  {q:"How much of the field's corners were to be reaped?", options:["Not the whole; the corners were to be left","Every corner, completely","Only the east corner","Half of each corner"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 19:9",verse:"And when ye reap the harvest of your land, thou shalt not wholly reap the corners of thy field, neither shalt thou gather the gleanings of thy harvest.",
   insight:"God built generosity into the harvest itself. Provision for the poor was not charity but law."},
  {q:"For whom were the gleanings of the harvest and vineyard to be left?", options:["For the poor and the stranger","For the priests and Levites","For the king's storehouses","For the widows of the tribe only"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 19:10",verse:"And thou shalt not glean thy vineyard, neither shalt thou gather every grape of thy vineyard; thou shalt leave them for the poor and stranger: I am the LORD your God.",
   insight:"The field's edges fed the marginalized. God's economy always leaves something for the needy."},
  {q:"What three dishonesties are forbidden in one verse?", options:["Stealing, dealing falsely, and lying to one another","Cheating, gambling, and bribery","Robbery, extortion, and fraud","Hoarding, lending at interest, and foreclosure"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 19:11",verse:"Ye shall not steal, neither deal falsely, neither lie one to another.",
   insight:"Stealing takes, false dealing twists, lying covers. Honesty must run through hands, deals, and words."},
  {q:"What is forbidden regarding God's name in an oath?", options:["Swearing falsely by His name and profaning it","Using His name in songs","Speaking His name aloud","Writing His name on doorposts"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 19:12",verse:"And ye shall not swear by my name falsely, neither shalt thou profane the name of thy God: I am the LORD.",
   insight:"A false oath drags God's name into a lie. His name is not a tool for our schemes."},
  {q:"How long could a hired servant's wages be kept?", options:["Not all night; they were due by morning","Until the end of the week","Until the next new moon","For thirty days"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 19:13",verse:"Thou shalt not defraud thy neighbour, neither rob him: the wages of him that is hired shall not abide with thee all night until the morning.",
   insight:"Withholding a day's wage was defrauding a neighbor. God notices when the powerful delay the powerless."},
  {q:"What kindness is commanded toward the deaf and the blind?", options:["Do not curse the deaf, nor put a stumblingblock before the blind","Give them the best seats in the assembly","Exempt them from all work","Pay them double wages"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 19:14",verse:"Thou shalt not curse the deaf, nor put a stumblingblock before the blind, but shalt fear thy God: I am the LORD.",
   insight:"God defends those who cannot defend themselves. Cruelty to the vulnerable is contempt for their Maker."},
  {q:"How was judgment to be carried out?", options:["Without respect of persons, neither favoring the poor nor honoring the mighty","Always in favor of the poor","Always in favor of the mighty","By casting lots in every case"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 19:15",verse:"Ye shall do no unrighteousness in judgment: thou shalt not respect the person of the poor, nor honour the person of the mighty: but in righteousness shalt thou judge thy neighbour.",
   insight:"Justice blind to status cuts both ways: no favoritism for the poor, no honor for the powerful. Righteousness alone decides."},
  {q:"What is forbidden concerning a neighbor's reputation and life?", options:["Going as a talebearer, and standing against the blood of a neighbor","Repeating any true story about him","Warning others about his faults","Refusing to trade with him"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 19:16",verse:"Thou shalt not go up and down as a talebearer among thy people: neither shalt thou stand against the blood of thy neighbour: I am the LORD.",
   insight:"Gossip and standing by while a neighbor is harmed are twins. Love guards both reputation and life."},
  {q:"What should be done instead of hating a brother in the heart?", options:["Rebuke the neighbor plainly, and not suffer sin upon him","Avoid him completely","Report him to the elders at once","Pray for him silently and say nothing"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 19:17",verse:"Thou shalt not hate thy brother in thine heart: thou shalt in any wise rebuke thy neighbour, and not suffer sin upon him.",
   insight:"Silent hatred poisons; honest rebuke heals. Love speaks the hard truth instead of nursing the grudge."},
  {q:"Which famous commandment appears in verse 18?", options:["Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself","Thou shalt love the LORD thy God","Thou shalt do unto others","Thou shalt forgive seventy times seven"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 19:18",verse:"Thou shalt not avenge, nor bear any grudge against the children of thy people, but thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself: I am the LORD.",
   insight:"Long before the Gospels, the law summed neighbor love in one line. Jesus quoted Leviticus."},
  {q:"What mixtures were forbidden in field, flock, and clothing?", options:["Diverse kinds of cattle, mingled seed, and garments of linen and wool mixed","Wheat and barley, goats and sheep, cotton and silk","Oxen and donkeys, vines and figs, blue and purple","Horses and camels, rye and oats, leather and linen"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 19:19",verse:"Ye shall keep my statutes. Thou shalt not let thy cattle gender with a diverse kind: thou shalt not sow thy field with mingled seed: neither shall a garment mingled of linen and woollen come upon thee.",
   insight:"God ordered His creation with boundaries. Honoring His distinctions is part of holiness."},
  {q:"Why were the man and the betrothed bondmaid not put to death?", options:["Because she was not free","Because they confessed quickly","Because the elders pleaded for them","Because it was their first offense"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 19:20",verse:"And whosoever lieth carnally with a woman, that is a bondmaid, betrothed to an husband, and not at all redeemed, nor freedom given her; she shall be scourged; they shall not be put to death, because she was not free.",
   insight:"God weighed circumstances with justice. Even in judgment, He considered what bound the guilty."},
  {q:"For how many years was a newly planted tree's fruit to be as uncircumcised and uneaten?", options:["Three years","One year","Seven years","Five years"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 19:23",verse:"And when ye shall come into the land, and shall have planted all manner of trees for food, then ye shall count the fruit thereof as uncircumcised: three years shall it be as uncircumcised unto you: it shall not be eaten of.",
   insight:"Patience was planted with the trees. The firstfruits of waiting belong to God."},
  {q:"What was the fourth year's fruit to be?", options:["Holy, to praise the LORD withal","Given entirely to the priests","Burned as an offering","Stored for famine years"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 19:24",verse:"But in the fourth year all the fruit thereof shall be holy to praise the LORD withal.",
   insight:"The fourth year turned harvest into worship. Increase is meant to end in praise."},
  {q:"What two practices are forbidden in verse 26?", options:["Eating anything with the blood, and using enchantment or observing times","Eating pork, and consulting the stars","Drinking wine, and dancing at feasts","Working on feast days, and traveling on Sabbaths"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 19:26",verse:"Ye shall not eat any thing with the blood: neither shall ye use enchantment, nor observe times.",
   insight:"Blood belonged to God, and the future belonged to God. Both superstition and presumption were shut out."},
  {q:"What honor was due to the aged?", options:["Rise up before the hoary head, and honour the face of the old man","Give them the first portion of every sacrifice","Exempt them from the law's penalties","Seat them at the city gate as judges"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 19:32",verse:"Thou shalt rise up before the hoary head, and honour the face of the old man, and fear thy God: I am the LORD.",
   insight:"Standing for the elderly taught a generation to honor God. Reverence is learned in small gestures."}
 ]},
{n:20, name:"Punishments for Sin",
 seal:{ref:"Leviticus 20:26",verse:"And ye shall be holy unto me: for I the LORD am holy, and have severed you from other people, that ye should be mine."},
 facts:["Did you know this chapter's penalties run from stoning to burning to being 'cut off,' showing how seriously God guarded worship, family, and purity (Leviticus 20:2, 14, 18)?","Did you know the phrase 'their blood shall be upon them' repeats through this chapter, teaching that the sinner's guilt rests on himself, not on God (Leviticus 20:9-13)?","Did you know the chapter ends not with punishment but with belonging: God severed Israel from other peoples so they would be His own (Leviticus 20:26)?"],
 questions:[
  {q:"What was the penalty for giving one's seed to Molech?", options:["He was put to death; the people of the land stoned him","He was cut off from the camp","He paid a ransom of a hundred shekels","He was sold as a servant"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 20:2",verse:"Again, thou shalt say to the children of Israel, Whosoever he be of the children of Israel, or of the strangers that sojourn in Israel, that giveth any of his seed unto Molech; he shall surely be put to death: the people of the land shall stone him with stones.",
   insight:"Child sacrifice met the stones of the whole community. God enlisted His people to hate what He hates."},
  {q:"Why would God cut off the Molech worshipper from his people?", options:["Because he defiled God's sanctuary and profaned His holy name","Because he wasted the nation's wealth","Because he dishonored his ancestors","Because he broke the Sabbath"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 20:3",verse:"And I will set my face against that man, and will cut him off from among his people; because he hath given of his seed unto Molech, to defile my sanctuary, and to profane my holy name.",
   insight:"Idolatry was not private sin; it defiled God's house and smeared His name. Worship has public consequences."},
  {q:"What did God threaten if the people hid their eyes from the man who gave his seed to Molech?", options:["He would set His face against that man and his family","He would forgive them all","He would send a plague on the camp","He would appoint new judges over them"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 20:5",verse:"Then I will set my face against that man, and against his family, and will cut him off, and all that go a whoring after him, to commit whoredom with Molech, from among their people.",
   insight:"Looking away from evil made the people partners in it. Tolerated sin spreads to the family."},
  {q:"What became of the soul that turned to familiar spirits and wizards?", options:["God set His face against that soul and cut him off","He was fined ten shekels","He was unclean for a month","He was sent to the priests for teaching"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 20:6",verse:"And the soul that turneth after such as have familiar spirits, and after wizards, to go a whoring after them, I will even set my face against that soul, and will cut him off from among his people.",
   insight:"Seeking the dead instead of God was spiritual adultery. God called it whoring after them."},
  {q:"What does God command before listing the penalties?", options:["Sanctify yourselves, and be ye holy","Gather the elders together","Build an altar of stones","Number the fighting men"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 20:7",verse:"Sanctify yourselves therefore, and be ye holy: for I am the LORD your God.",
   insight:"Judgment and holiness walk together. The penalties exist because God is holy and wants His people holy."},
  {q:"Who is it that sanctifies Israel?", options:["The LORD: 'I am the LORD which sanctify you'","The high priest alone","Moses the lawgiver","The elders of the tribes"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 20:8",verse:"And ye shall keep my statutes, and do them: I am the LORD which sanctify you.",
   insight:"Holiness is not self-made. God commands it and God produces it."},
  {q:"What was the penalty for cursing father or mother?", options:["He was surely put to death; his blood was upon him","He was beaten with stripes","He was disinherited","He fasted forty days"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 20:9",verse:"For every one that curseth his father or his mother shall be surely put to death: he hath cursed his father or his mother; his blood shall be upon him.",
   insight:"'His blood shall be upon him': the guilt rested on the curser, not on God. Sin's wages are earned."},
  {q:"What was the penalty for adultery with a neighbor's wife?", options:["Both the adulterer and the adulteress were put to death","Only the man was punished","They were divorced and fined","They were driven from the city"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 20:10",verse:"And the man that committeth adultery with another man’s wife, even he that committeth adultery with his neighbour’s wife, the adulterer and the adulteress shall surely be put to death.",
   insight:"Both parties bore the penalty. God refused the double standard."},
  {q:"What was the penalty for lying with his father's wife?", options:["Both were surely put to death; their blood was upon them","They were cut off but lived","The man was exiled","The woman was divorced"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 20:11",verse:"And the man that lieth with his father’s wife hath uncovered his father’s nakedness: both of them shall surely be put to death; their blood shall be upon them.",
   insight:"Violating a father's marriage struck at the family's foundation. The penalty matched the wreckage."},
  {q:"What was the penalty for a man lying with mankind as with a woman?", options:["Both were put to death; they had committed an abomination","They were cut off from the people","They paid a heavy fine","They were shamed publicly"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 20:13",verse:"If a man also lie with mankind, as he lieth with a woman, both of them have committed an abomination: they shall surely be put to death; their blood shall be upon them.",
   insight:"God's word does not waver with the times. He calls it abomination and means it."},
  {q:"What was the penalty for a man who took both a wife and her mother?", options:["They were burnt with fire","They were stoned","They were exiled","They were cut off"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 20:14",verse:"And if a man take a wife and her mother, it is wickedness: they shall be burnt with fire, both he and they; that there be no wickedness among you.",
   insight:"Fire marked the sin as utterly corrupting. Some evils must be purged, not managed."},
  {q:"What happened to the man who lay with a beast, and to the beast?", options:["The man was put to death and the beast was slain","The man was fined and the beast freed","The man was exiled and the beast sacrificed","The man was beaten and the beast sold"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 20:15",verse:"And if a man lie with a beast, he shall surely be put to death: and ye shall slay the beast.",
   insight:"Even the animal was slain, so no trace of the confusion remained. God purges sin thoroughly."},
  {q:"What happened to a woman who approached a beast?", options:["Both the woman and the beast were killed","Only the woman was punished","She was cut off but lived","She was sold into servitude"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 20:16",verse:"And if a woman approach unto any beast, and lie down thereto, thou shalt kill the woman, and the beast: they shall surely be put to death; their blood shall be upon them.",
   insight:"The law spared neither sex. Holiness bound men and women alike."},
  {q:"What became of a man who took his sister?", options:["They were cut off in the sight of their people","They were secretly fined","They were warned once","They were sent away quietly"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 20:17",verse:"And if a man shall take his sister, his father’s daughter, or his mother’s daughter, and see her nakedness, and she see his nakedness; it is a wicked thing; and they shall be cut off in the sight of their people: he hath uncovered his sister’s nakedness; he shall bear his iniquity.",
   insight:"'In the sight of their people': the cutting off was public. Open sin met open judgment."},
  {q:"What became of both who lay together during her sickness?", options:["Both were cut off from among their people","Both were unclean seven days","Both offered a sin offering","Both were beaten"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 20:18",verse:"And if a man shall lie with a woman having her sickness, and shall uncover her nakedness; he hath discovered her fountain, and she hath uncovered the fountain of her blood: and both of them shall be cut off from among their people.",
   insight:"Even this drew the severest covenant penalty. God guards the intimate realm closely."},
  {q:"What did those who uncovered an aunt's nakedness bear?", options:["Their iniquity","A fine of silver","Seven years of exile","Public shame only"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 20:19",verse:"And thou shalt not uncover the nakedness of thy mother’s sister, nor of thy father’s sister: for he uncovereth his near kin: they shall bear their iniquity.",
   insight:"To 'bear iniquity' is to carry the guilt. Some sins are not punished at once but carried."},
  {q:"What was the consequence for lying with his uncle's wife?", options:["They would die childless","They were stoned","They were cut off","They paid double dowry"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 20:20",verse:"And if a man shall lie with his uncle’s wife, he hath uncovered his uncle’s nakedness: they shall bear their sin; they shall die childless.",
   insight:"Childlessness struck at the future. Sin can forfeit what we hoped to pass on."},
  {q:"What was the consequence for taking his brother's wife?", options:["They would be childless","They were burnt with fire","They were exiled","They were publicly shamed"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 20:21",verse:"And if a man shall take his brother’s wife, it is an unclean thing: he hath uncovered his brother’s nakedness; they shall be childless.",
   insight:"Again the line ends. God showed how seriously He guards marriage bonds."},
  {q:"Why must Israel keep all God's statutes and judgments?", options:["So the land would not spue them out","So their crops would double","So the nations would fear them","So the temple would stand forever"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 20:22",verse:"Ye shall therefore keep all my statutes, and all my judgments, and do them: that the land, whither I bring you to dwell therein, spue you not out.",
   insight:"Disobedience could cost them the land itself. Blessing has an address: obedience."},
  {q:"What had God done to set Israel apart?", options:["He severed them from other people, that they should be His","He gave them a king like the nations","He built them a wall of fire","He taught them the languages of nations"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 20:26",verse:"And ye shall be holy unto me: for I the LORD am holy, and have severed you from other people, that ye should be mine.",
   insight:"Severed: cut away and claimed. Holiness is belonging to God, set apart for Him alone."}
 ]}
]);
